WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.120
Film Courage: Have you ever given up on a
script because you’ve lost interest?

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.550
Gary Goldstein: Here and there…you know
I generally don’t start writing something

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:13.070
unless I’ve done outlining and enough exploration
of the idea to know it’s worth going to

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:14.070
script.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.750
Particularly if I am writing somethings on
spec, there are definitely things I’ve written

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:20.980
on spec that…a few things I started and
for some reason I’ve just sort of abandoned

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:26.200
because I felt like I can go back and finish
this sometime if I want to.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.220
But it’s not great enough, it’s not going
to be unique enough, so it becomes a bit of

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.220
an exercise.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:36.309
But by and large I’ve always tried not to
start writing something unless it felt like

00:00:36.309 --> 00:00:40.960
something I was going to be able to finish
or that it was really worth doing.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.780
It’s like reading a book.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.429
It’s like if I go to the trouble of starting
to read a book, I might as well finish it,

00:00:46.429 --> 00:00:47.489
you know?

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:48.969
But…unless an idea is terrible.

00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:51.370
We don’t always have great ideas.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:52.909
Sometimes there is just not enough there.

00:00:52.909 --> 00:01:00.320
But I usually can know that in the outline
stage to save me the hardship of writing the

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.600
script and having to sort of like squash it.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.380
Film Courage: So what if you meet with writer
who is new to the industry or baby writer

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:15.340
(whatever you want to call them) and they
just have a slew of unfinished scripts?

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.240
Gary Goldstein: Well there’s a reason you
have a slew of unfinished scripts.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:25.409
Especially if you are starting off writing,
you need one or two good solid finished scripts.

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:30.261
So when I hear somebody has a slew of unfinished
scripts, it means that they are not really

00:01:30.261 --> 00:01:32.680
picking and choosing their stories well.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.520
That they are not passionate enough about
the story or they are cheating themselves

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.539
out of what the story is about or they are
just trying to write a thriller just because

00:01:41.539 --> 00:01:45.110
they think it is going to sell or something
like that.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:48.609
And a lot of people start writing without
having the necessary tools.

00:01:48.609 --> 00:01:50.740
I think everybody thinks “You know, I can
write.”

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.920
But they don’t really understand structure.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.960
I’m not saying you have to take a course
in it but you have to have done your homework

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:06.219
in one way or another and understand how to
tell a story cinematically or for television

00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:07.560
and not everybody has done that.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.479
Some people just jump in and they’re not
really ready.

